    "question": "How do I query Transfer events of a token on Pharos?",
    "docs": {
      "question": "How do I query Transfer events of a token on Pharos?",
      "answer": "Use `eth_getLogs` with the token contract address and the ERC-20 `Transfer(address,address,uint256)` event signature as `topics[0]`.\n\n```bash\ncurl https://rpc.pharos.xyz \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n    \"method\": \"eth_getLogs\",\n    \"params\": [{\n      \"fromBlock\": \"0x100000\",\n      \"toBlock\": \"0x100100\",\n      \"address\": \"<TOKEN_CONTRACT_ADDRESS>\",\n      \"topics\": [\"0xddf252ad1be2c89b69c2b068fc378daa952ba7f163c4a11628f55a4df523b3ef\"]\n    }],\n    \"id\": 1\n  }'\n```\n\nReplace the block range and token address. Each returned log includes `topics` and `data`; for standard ERC-20 transfers, the indexed sender and recipient are in `topics[1]` and `topics[2]`, while the amount is in `data`.\n\nFor one address, add its 32-byte, zero-padded address as the second or third topic to filter by sender or recipient. For live events, use `eth_subscribe` with the `logs` filter over WebSocket.\n\nPharos limits `eth_getLogs` ranges to 100 blocks when rate limiting is enabled, or 10,000 blocks otherwise. Split larger historical queries into smaller ranges. See [JSON-RPC API Methods](https://docs.pharos.xyz/api-and-sdk/json-rpc-methods) for the filter format and [Pharos MCP](https://docs.pharos.xyz/tooling-and-infrastructure/mcp) for event-log query limits.",
